hare":0.0,"_gate_rev_durability":0.09426108253995613,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":72.1,"_gate_fcf_durability":0.04241305360063907,"_gp_fcf_durability":false,"_score_fcf_durability":46.2,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":0.0,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0869032656031661,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":100.0,"_gate_share_shrink":0.0023546757542585173,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":47.1,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.013218233945282345,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":78.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.36588,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Western Midstream Partners, LP, together with its subsidiaries, operates as a midstream energy company primarily in the United States. The company is involved in gathering, compressing, treating, processing, and transporting natural gas; gathering, stabilizing, and transporting condensate, natural gas liquids (NGLs), and crude oil; and gathering and disposing of produced water. It also buys and sells residue, NGLs, and condensates. The company operates assets located in Texas, New Mexico, and the Rocky Mountains. It also provides water handling solutions. The company was formerly known as Western Gas Equity Partners, LP and changed its name to Western Midstream Partners, LP in February 2019. Western Midstream Partners, LP was incorporated in 2007 and is based in The Woodlands, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Oscar K. Brown | President, CEO & Director - Western Midstream Holdings LLC | Age 54 | Compensation: $2.9M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $2.3M ranks in the above the sector median among Energy peers, improving at 11%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $877K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $30K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity).","financial_summary":["Western Midstream Partners, LP is a large-cap energy business with a $16.5B market cap on $3.8B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +6% over the past three years and +9% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","The business throws off $1.5B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 39 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.88\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.2\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 4.1\u00d7.","Management returns a meaningful 8.7% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 37% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and within its sector the business captures 3.8\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.6) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Carries a 56% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.5640275135372457},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Energy tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"The commodity cycle \u2014 revenue and free cash flow are ultimately hostage to a price the company does not set, and the long-run energy-transition arc adds a structural overhang.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"ESG-driven capital flight and stranded asset risk \u2014 tightening climate policy and investor mandates raise the cost of capital and risk leaving reserves permanently in the ground.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Geopolitical supply shocks \u2014 wars, sanctions, and OPEC decisions create volatility that no hedging programme can fully neutralise.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 26% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","13 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk","Energy sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 6% over 3 months","Energy ETF in a sustained uptrend above its long-term average"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Which Wesfarmers (ASX:WES) Divisions Softened as Rates Bite?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/bluechip/which-wesfarmers-asxwes-divisions-softened-as-rates-bite","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793926920.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.128,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Is Wesfarmers' (ASX:WES) Lithium Turnaround Finally Taking Shape?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/bluechip/is-wesfarmers-asxwes-lithium-turnaround-finally-taking-shape","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793925480.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"